  <!-- 3. DELTA: What Moved (Last 24-48h) -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On July 7–8, 2026, IRGC naval forces struck three commercial tankers in the Strait of Hormuz — the crude carriers Al Rekayat and Wedyan and a third unnamed VLCC — with anti-ship missiles and maritime drone swarms. UKMTO issued a Corridor 2 Avoidance Notice at 03:41 UTC on July 8; Bimco confirmed all three vessels sustained hull damage, with crew sheltering in citadels and zero fatalities reported. CENTCOM formally designated the strikes as a deliberate violation of the June 17 MOU Annex B shipping protection clauses. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>CENTCOM retaliated within six hours, destroying two IRGC fast-attack craft at Bandar Abbas and a coastal radar installation at Larak Island per official CENTCOM statement July 8. Iranian armed forces subsequently announced retaliatory strikes against US pre-positioning facilities at Camp Arifjan (Kuwait) and Al-Udeid forward operating base (Bahrain) via Fateh-110 ballistic missiles — the first confirmed Iran-to-US-base strikes since March 2020; DoD reported five personnel with non-critical injuries. US Secretary of Defense Austin reimposed all secondary oil sanctions at 09:00 UTC July 8. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>The June 17 MOU between the United States and Iran established a 60-day technical negotiations window closing August 16, 2026. Vance confirmed at a July 1 press briefing that the Doha channel — established June 30–July 1 — remained the active track for detailed nuclear protocols; no formal session had been scheduled in the July 1–6 period before the July 7–8 exchange. The July 7–8 incident is the second material breach of the MOU shipping annex; the first (June 23 IRGC tanker incident) was resolved through the Doha channel without cancelling the formal session track. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Knesset Legal Adviser Sagit Afik issued a formal dissolution recommendation on July 7, specifying July 17 as the earliest constitutionally permissible dissolution date; Speaker Amir Ohana and Coalition Whip David Katz endorsed the timetable. Degel HaTorah filed a formal dissolution motion on July 8 — the second F7 indicator now OBSERVED. Religious Zionism (Smotrich) announced internal party primaries for July 26, confirming treatment of dissolution as certain. The confirmed election date is October 27, 2026, the statutory maximum for a July 17 dissolution under the 90–150 day window. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span></p>

      <p>IMF PortWatch recorded daily commercial Hormuz transits of 43, 34, and 31 on July 3–5, a declining trajectory in the days before the July 7–8 attacks. AXSMarine reported a pre-July 7 running average of approximately 35–40 transits/day as the recovery baseline established after the June 17 MOU reopening. Six major P&amp;I clubs (Gard, Standard, UK P&amp;I, West of England, Britannia, North P&amp;I) had already maintained the Hormuz exclusion zone; after July 7–8, Lloyd's war-risk premiums reached approximately eight times pre-crisis rates. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

